<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fosse Way</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fosse Way was a Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> running about 370 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> to Lincoln. Built in the first decades after the conquest of 43 CE, it marked for a time the western limit of Roman control. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> fossa, a ditch. Much of its straight line is still followed by modern roads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fosse-way.jpg" alt="Fosse Way" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neddyseagoon at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fosse_Way.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
